WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.344
[MUSIC]

00:00:08.344 --> 00:00:10.720
Conflict is costly for the economy.

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:13.950
People when they think about conflict,
they often think about the emotive parts

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:18.450
of conflict like the visceral images that
we get on television and things like that.

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:22.870
And often the economic side that
we all benefit when there's peace.

00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:27.310
And we all lose when there's conflict
is often kind of thrown by the wayside.

00:00:27.310 --> 00:00:31.230
But we thought, well, maybe if we
expose people to the opportunity of

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:36.420
trading stocks, this might make them more
aware of the kind of the gains to society.

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:38.310
And might change their voting behavior and

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:41.260
their attitudes towards
the peace process as well.

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:42.885
And that's exactly what we found.

00:00:47.633 --> 00:00:52.120
What we found was that being supposed
to maybe $50 to $100 worth of stock.

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:53.260
Even that exposure for

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:57.720
a period of four to seven weeks was
enough to change people's attitudes.

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:00.610
To help them become more
financially literate, and

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:04.200
to change their voting decisions
in a fairly substantial way.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:06.930
People were really
changing their votes and

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:11.440
their attitudes towards the peace process
based on the gains to Israel's economy.

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:13.700
So it's really a kind
of sociotropic thing.

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:17.740
I think that people will begin
to appreciate that there's this

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:20.290
common good that's the Israeli economy.

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:23.950
Stock markets potentially
are an imperfect but

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:27.580
nonetheless a nonpartisan
gauge of that economy.

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:31.921
And by being exposed to the stock market,
they get this nonpartisan metric for

00:01:31.921 --> 00:01:34.640
measuring and
evaluating different policies.

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:40.305
[MUSIC]

00:01:40.305 --> 00:01:43.984
Finance I think is particularly promising
in many ways because it's really about

00:01:43.984 --> 00:01:45.110
sharing the future..

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:48.820
This idea of using financial
instruments to really have our people

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:53.450
share in the gains from society as
well as incentivize them to work for

00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:58.540
the common good, is really a powerful
idea that isn't just restricted to firms.

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:02.530
It's also something that we
could do collectively as well.

00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:05.460
If we are all invested in the market
portfolio or index funds,

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:07.890
we all benefit when the economy benefits.

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:12.738
This focus on the common good, this
incentive to support the common good, and

00:02:12.738 --> 00:02:17.513
this nonpartisan metric about what
the common good is can really, we think,

00:02:17.513 --> 00:02:21.341
be a very prudent way to mitigate
polarization and conflict.

00:02:21.341 --> 00:02:29.907
[MUSIC]

